    "evidence_text": "When a sentence is given and encoded into a vector by the encoder, the decoders are trained to predict the next sentence and the previous sentence.\n\nInferSent  instead pre-trains the model in a manner of supervised learning.\n\npre-training task to learn the sentence representation.\n\nMethod\tOur method follows the same input format and the model architecture with original BERT.\n\nThe NSP task is a binary classification task, which takes two sentences (A and B) as input and determines whether B is the next sentence of A. Although it has been proven to be very effective for BERT, there are two major deficiencies.\n\nAs reported , the final pre-trained model is able to achieve 97%-98% accuracy on the NSP task.\n\nNote that these in-adjacent sentences will introduce much more training noise to the model.\n\n0.0) to (0.8, 0.2) in a binary classification problem.\n\nIn summary, when A is given, the pre-training example for each label is constructed as follows: IsNext: Choosing the adjacent following sentence as B. IsPrev: Choosing the adjacent previous sentence as B. IsNextInadj: Choosing the in-adjacent following sentence as B. There is a sentence between A and B. IsPrevInadj: Choosing the in-adjacent previous sentence as B. There is a sentence between A and B. DiffDoc: Drawing B randomly from a different document.\n\nExperiment Settings\tThis section gives detailed experiment settings.\n\nThe method is evaluated on the BERTbase model, which has 12 layers, 12 self-attention heads with a hidden size of 768.\n\nTo accelerate the training speed, two-phase training  is adopted.\n\nThe first phase uses a maximal sentence length of 128, and 512 for the second phase.\n\nThe numbers of training steps of two phases are 50K and 40K for the BERTBase model.\n\nWe used AdamW  optimizer with a learning rate of 1e-4, a $\\beta _1$ of\n\n0.9, a $\\beta _2$ of 0.999 and a L2 weight decay rate of $0.01$. The first 10% of the total steps are used for learning rate warming up, followed by the linear decay schema. We used a dropout probability of 0.1 on all layers. The data used for pre-training is the same as BERT, i.e., English Wikipedia (2500M words) and BookCorpus (800M words) . For the Masked LM task, we followed the same masking rate and settings as in BERT. We explore three method settings for comparison. BERT-PN: The NSP task in BERT is replaced by a 3-class task with IsNext, IsPrev and DiffDoc. The label distribution is 1:1:1. BERT-PN5cls: The NSP task in BERT is replaced by a 5-class task with two additional labels IsNextInadj, IsPrevInadj. The label distribution is 1:1:1:1:1. BERT-PNsmth: It uses the same data with BERT-PN5cls, except that the IsPrevInadj\n\n(IsNextInadj) label is mapped to IsPrev (IsNext) with a label smoothing factor of 0.8.\n\nThe order of the input pair of NLI samples is reversed in the fine-tuning phase, and other hyper-parameters and settings keep the same with the BERT paper.\n\nwas conducted for 3 epochs for all the tasks, with a learning rate of 2e-5.\n\nThe predictions were obtained by evaluating the training checkpoint with the best validation performance.\n\nThe model computes for a concatenation a scalar as the score, which is then used in a softmax layer for the final prediction.\n\nThe fine-tuning was conducted for 5 epochs, with a batch size of 32 and a learning rate of\n\nFor Chinese NLP tasks, we pre-train the model using Chinese corpus.\n\nFor the first 3 Chinese tasks, we follow the settings as in ERNIE .\n\nThe proposed method is compared with four models, i.e., BERTBase , BERTBase with whole word masking , ERNIE  and ERNIE 2.0 .\n\nNote that the Chinese ERNIE 2.0 is equipped with 5 different\n\nNSP task to provide more document-level information in the pre-training.\n\nMotivated by the in-symmetric property of NSP, we propose to differentiate between different sentence orders by including PSP.\n\nDespite the simplicity, extensive experiments demonstrate that the model obtains a better ability in pair-wise semantic reasoning.\n\nOur work suggests that the document-level objective is effective, at least for the BERTbase model.\n\nIn the future, we will investigate the way to take advantages of both large-scale training and our method.",
